s Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "A transient burst of in-flight reservations marks a key no_room for the whole billing period, with no self-recovery", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It breaks because the low-room test measures room net of open reservations, which are transient estimates, but the mark it sets lasts the whole period. In KeyPool._refused_for_room (pool.py), a refused reservation snapshots the account and computes remaining = budget - spent - reserved (_money_of). When that is below min_headroom it writes generation['low_room'] = period. _blocks then treats the key as NO_ROOM until rollover. The only paths that clear the mark are an operator reset, a period rollover, or _mark_rooms. _mark_rooms runs only inside a _commit whose edit produces a move (target != before). So once no other key is eligible, nothing ever re-reads room. Reservations are deliberately pessimistic: max_tokens, or the model's max_output_tokens when the call sets none, priced at the output rate. That makes reserved near the budget a normal state under concurrency, and mixed call sizes routinely leave the last gap under 1%. Example: a $10 key, four in-flight $2.45 reservations plus one $0.15. The next refused call sees $0.05 remaining and marks the key low for the month. Its spend after those calls settle may be a few cents. In a one-key pool, every later call raises NoKeyAvailableError until month end or a manual reset. In an n-key pool the same happens once the last eligible key is marked: its _commit computes no target, so it never reads room and never clears the marks on keys that recovered long ago. status() then shows a large 'remaining' beside state no_room. The operator page's advice (raise the budget, redeploy, reset) treats a load spike as a sizing mistake. No test reserves concurrently and then checks the low-room mark (test_a_key_below_headroom_is_moved_off uses settled spend only). Fix: decide low room on settled spend, which within a period only grows, so a period-long mark is true. Alternatively, re-read room before treating a low_room mark as blocking (at least before raising NoKeyAvailableError). Pin the fix with a test that fills a key with open reservations, settles them low, and expects the key to serve again.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py", "packages/models/tests/unit/models/test_key_pool_selection.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A key's low_room mark is set from room net of open reservations but cleared only by a move's room read, a reset or a rollover.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:KeyPool._refused_for_room"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:KeyPool._commit (rooms read only when a move is computed)"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "With distinct priorities, a key forbidden for one model makes the shared active key thrash on mixed-model traffic", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It breaks because a per-model ineligibility drives the pool-wide active pointer. In _keep_active_eligible, _target computes blocks for the call's model_id. Take a pool with A at priority 1 and B at priority 2, where A is forbidden for model X only. A model-X call finds A ineligible and commits a move A->B (reason forbidden, logged at WARNING). The next model-Y call finds A eligible and more preferred, so StickySelection.active_key pulls back B->A (reason pull_back, INFO). Every alternation between X and Y calls costs a record compare-and-set plus a snapshot of every key's account, which breaks KP-G6's two-operations success path. It also logs a fresh WARNING 'moved its active key ... forbidden' on each model-X call and climbs the moves counter. Across processes, those writes contend on the one record. A pool is per provider and serves every model, so a consumer running two models on one pool with priorities set hits this whenever one key lacks access to one model. Fix: a block that applies to one model only (forbidden for a model id) should skip the key for that call, as KP-D3's skip does, rather than move or pull back the active key. Moves and pull-backs should be decided on whole-key states. Add a test that alternates two models over that pool and asserts no moves after the first.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/selection.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "Active-key moves and pull-backs computed from blocks that hold only for the calling model.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:KeyPool._keep_active_eligible / KeyPool._target"}], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A per-model forbid moves the pool-wide active key, so mixed-model traffic with distinct priorities flaps it on every call", "recommendation": "The pool has one active key per pool, shared by every process and by every model the pool serves (KP-D1). But KeyPool._target, which _keep_active_eligible runs on every acquire and _commit runs on every write, decides that key from _all_blocks(doc, model_id, now): eligibility for the model of the call in hand. Take one pool serving models X and Y, with primary at priority 0 and overflow at 1, and primary forbidden for X only (a per-model 403, VerdictScope.KEY_AND_MODEL). An X call finds primary ineligible and commits a move primary->overflow, logged at WARNING as 'forbidden'. The next Y call finds primary eligible and more preferred, and commits a move overflow->primary as pull_back (StickySelection.active_key). This repeats on every X/Y alternation. Each move costs a room snapshot of every key's account plus a compare-and-set of the record, which with BudgetLedger is a synchronous L3 write. Each move also logs a line and bumps threetears_key_pool_moves_total. That breaks KP-G6, which allows two store operations on the success path. Across processes the flapping writers contend for one record, and _MAX_CAS_ROUNDS (16) lost rounds fail the call closed with KeyPoolStoreError. With equal priorities there is no flapping, but one model's forbid still moves every other model's traffic onto the overflow key's budget. KP-D2's '(for this model)' plus KP-D5's pull-back is where it comes from, and no test runs two models through one pool with distinct priorities. Suggested fix: decide the active key from key-wide state only (_target with model_id=None), and let a per-model forbid only skip the key for that call. serving_key already does that from the cached record, with no store call, much as KP-D3 skips a key for room. This changes the KP-D2 requirement, so record the owner's decision and amend KP-D2 in docs/model-key-pool-requirements.md alongside the code. Then add a test that interleaves two models over a distinct-priority pool and asserts that no move and no record write happens after the first. Scope: instance. One decision site, the model_id that _target passes to _all_blocks, is why it broke.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py", "docs/model-key-pool-requirements.md"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A site is a member when it chooses the pool-wide active key from eligibility computed for one call's model rather than from key-wide state.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:KeyPool._target (via _keep_active_eligible and _commit)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "docs/model-key-pool-requirements.md:KP-D2 '(for this model)'"}], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A record the pool can't decode fails the call closed without the ERROR line or the store-failure count an operator alerts on", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: only failures raised inside KeyPool._call (and the CAS-exhaustion branch of _commit) are logged at ERROR and reported through on_store_failure. Record-decoding failures raised after a store call has returned skip both. Members: _checked_doc, called from _read and from _commit on the compare-and-set result, raises KeyPoolStoreError for a record in another layout (a mixed-version rollout, once a layout 2 exists); _generation_number raises KeyPoolStoreError when the record lost a key during registration; and a malformed field (datetime.fromisoformat in _exhausted_until, Decimal(stored_budget) in _register) raises an unwrapped ValueError/InvalidOperation. Each one fails every call on the pool, yet threetears_key_pool_store_failures_total stays at 0 and the pool logs nothing. docs/model-key-pool.md section 'Choosing a store' tells operators a store failure 'is logged at ERROR and counted', so an operator alerting on that counter misses a pool that refuses every call. test_a_record_in_another_layout_is_refused_not_overwritten checks the raise but not the count. Fix with one construction, not a list: decode the record inside the _call boundary (for example, pass a decode step to _call for read_record and compare_and_set_record), so every failure to read the record goes through the one logged, counted path. Then assert the count in the layout test.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py", "packages/models/tests/unit/models/test_key_pool_failure.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A failure to read or interpret the pool's stored record that raises outside KeyPool._call, so it is neither logged at ERROR nor reported through on_store_failure.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:_checked_doc (via _read and _commit)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:_generation_number"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:_exhausted_until (datetime.fromisoformat)"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:_register (Decimal(stored_budget))"}], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The pool's own OpenRouter rate-limit retries sleep up to twice rate_limit_exhaustion_wait with no log line", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: KeyPool.wait_to_retry took over the job the Anthropic and OpenAI SDKs do for themselves, but not the SDKs' 'Retrying request ... in N seconds' INFO line. On OpenRouter a call can now spend up to 2 x 120 s asleep on the same key, and nothing records it. The pool logs no line, no counter moves (by the owner's ruling, a rate limit is not a key event), and the tracker logs the failed attempt only at DEBUG. An operator chasing a slow OpenRouter call has no trace of why it was slow. Add one INFO line in wait_to_retry before the sleep, naming the pool, the retry number and the wait. It stays a log line, not a key state, a move or a metric, so it doesn't conflict with the ruling.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The pool-side rate-limit retry in KeyPool.wait_to_retry.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py:KeyPool.wait_to_retry"}], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The plan's inferred decisions and two owner confirmations, all flagged for this boundary, merge with no recorded answer, including the persisted-format lock-ins", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the build plan's Goals section lists 'Decisions made before building (inferred; flag at the boundary)' marked owner-can-veto. Those are money as a Decimal stored as a string; the store Protocol in generic ledger terms; open reservations kept as a map inside the account row; and the two new coordination tables. Mid-build lines add two more items, 'Owner to confirm at the boundary': the BudgetLedger and KeyPoolMetrics entries in _public_api_consumers.txt that name Hearsay as the only caller. .prawduct/owner-rulings.md records no answer to any of them. Chunk 04 calls the account and record shapes the 'Persisted format (lock-in)', and v004 creates the tables the ledger writes them to. Once a release carries this, vetoing any of them takes a migration, not an edit. Before the merge, put them to the owner and record the answer, or record that they stand as inferred, with the reason.", "files": ["packages/core/src/threetears/core/coordination/budget_ledger.py", "packages/core/src/threetears/core/coordination/tables.py", "packages/core/src/threetears/core/coordination/migrations/v004_create_ledger_tables.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/store.py", "tests/enforcement/_public_api_consumers.txt"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The decisions the model-key-pool plan marks inferred or owner-to-confirm at the boundary.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "Decimal money stored as a string (budget_ledger.py, store.py)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "store Protocol in generic ledger terms (store.py KeyPoolStore)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "open reservations as a map in the account row (budget_ledger.py _AccountState)"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "coordination_ledger_accounts and coordination_documents tables (tables.py, v004)"}, {"id": "m5", "site": "_public_api_consumers.txt BudgetLedger and KeyPoolMetrics entries naming Hearsay"}], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: a third-party private member reached by overriding it in a src subclass can't be recorded in _underscore_exemptions.txt, as CLAUDE.md requires", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: CLAUDE.md's 'Leading underscores are a stability contract' section says that reaching into a third-party library's private members is 'recorded in tests/enforcement/_underscore_exemptions.txt with a specific rationale'. But no underscore_access shape flags a src subclass that overrides a third-party private member and reads super()._x: ruff's SLF001 skips super(), and shape I scans only tests/. An exemption line for such a reach would match nothing and read as stale. So providers/_langchain_anthropic_internals.py, which overrides ChatAnthropic._client_params, keeps its record only in its own docstring (build plan, chunk 03 decision). Anyone auditing third-party private reaches from the exemptions file, say before a langchain-anthropic upgrade, misses it, and the next override of this shape gets the same treatment. Mechanize it: add a src shape to threetears.enforcement.underscore_access that flags a class whose base comes from outside the threetears namespace when it defines or super()-reads a single-underscore member. That makes this reach, and every later one, take an exemption line that resolves.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/providers/_langchain_anthropic_internals.py", "tests/enforcement/_underscore_exemptions.txt"], "check": "rule-unenforced", "scope": "class", "closure": "A src class with a third-party base that overrides or super()-reads one of that base's single-underscore members, which no underscore_access shape detects, so it can't carry the exemption-file record CLAUDE.md requires.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "packages/models/src/threetears/models/providers/_langchain_anthropic_internals.py:SdkMiddlewareChatAnthropic._client_params"}], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint test-clock-import: packages/core/tests/integration/test_budget_ledger_live.py:43", "recommendation": "`datetime.datetime.now` runs when the module is imported, so no fixture can freeze it and every test in the process inherits the value; move it into a fixture or the test body, behind an injected clock or seeded generator; if it must stay, waive it on its line with `prawduct:allow prawduct/test-scope -- <why it cannot move>` (prawduct's docs/test-determinism.md)", "files": ["packages/core/tests/integration/test_budget_ledger_live.py"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-8"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "A key skipped for room is reconsidered on the call's next attempt", "recommendation": "KP-H2 says a key skipped for room counts as tried. KeyPool.acquire keeps `skipped` only within one acquire, and _Call.acquire passes only `tried`. So after a failover, a key skipped earlier in the same call is offered another reservation. That usually costs one refused store reserve, and if room has freed, the call runs there. It is harmless, but it differs from the requirement's wording. Either carry the skipped labels into _Call._tried or note the deviation in the requirement.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/pooled.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The pool record's persisted layout has no typed owner", "recommendation": "The cross-process record (layout 1) is a dict[str, Any], read and written by string key at about 38 sites in pool.py. Two constructors already build the key entry differently: _register uses {'budget': None, ...} and _writable_generation uses {'budget': str(budget), ...}. Reads use .get with defaults, while writes index directly (generation['forbidden'], generation['low_room'], doc['keys']). So a field missing from a stored record is silently defaulted on read, and on write raises a bare KeyError from inside _commit instead of KeyPoolStoreError. _checked_doc validates only 'layout' and 'keys'. Nothing reaches this today, since only this code writes layout 1. Before a layout 2 exists, consider one small codec: TypedDicts for the record, key entry and generation, with one decode/validate and one encode.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Small duplications across the key pool modules", "recommendation": "None of these is worth a round alone, so they are listed together: (1) _AnthropicClassifier, _OpenAIClassifier and _OpenRouterClassifier repeat the same classify/refuses_retry glue, differing only in _verdict, reports_quota and come_back (classify.py). One base parameterized by those would serve a fourth provider. (2) _rank is defined identically in pool.py and selection.py. (3) The verdict-to-MoveReason vocabulary is spelled three times: pool._VERDICT_REASON, pooled._FAILOVER_REASON and metrics._FAILOVER_REASONS, the last pinned by a test literal. (4) KeyPool.status() and KeyPool._no_key_available() each build a KeyStatus per key from the same snapshot and blocks loop.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/classify.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/selection.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/pooled.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/metrics.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "KeyPool.acquire's docstring still says the per-model breaker counts", "recommendation": "acquire's :param model_id: says 'its per-model states (forbidden, the per-model breaker) count'. Since 6e378d05a, _breaker_wait reads only the credential breaker, and its own docstring says the per-model rate-limit breaker is not read. Delete 'the per-model breaker' from acquire's docstring. A test already pins the behaviour, so this is prose only.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "KeyState mixes snapshot states with the per-call TRIED", "recommendation": "KeyState.TRIED exists only inside NoKeyAvailableError, so metrics.py has to filter it out (_SNAPSHOT_STATES), and any other consumer matching on KeyStatus.state from status() must know to do the same. If the enum is touched again, a separate per-call marker on the error's entries would keep status() typed to what it can return.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/status.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/metrics.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The provider SDK's 429-retry fact lives in two places", "recommendation": "Whether the provider's SDK retries a 429 is stated in two places: _OpenRouterClassifier.sdk_retries_rate_limits = False (classify.py), and create_openrouter_chat's 'del unretried_key_errors', which rests on the SDK retrying only 5XX. If the OpenRouter factory ever enables 429 retries, the pool and the SDK both retry. The Anthropic and OpenAI classifiers likewise restate their SDKs' behaviour. This is acceptable as is. A comment or test linking the two sites would make the dependency visible.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/classify.py", "packages/models/src/threetears/models/providers/openrouter.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "uuid-utils' dependency comment names only its older consumer", "recommendation": "packages/models/pyproject.toml's comment on uuid-utils still reads 'uuid7 message ids for a pooled CLI call (claude_cli_pool.LentClient)'. Since db61ec134, key_pool/pool.py imports uuid7 on the base install path for reservation ids. Make the comment relational ('uuid7 ids: claude_cli_pool, key_pool reservations') or drop the consumer list. Nothing reads the comment, but it is the note a dependency trim would consult.", "files": ["packages/models/pyproject.toml"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The models package README doesn't mention the key pool", "recommendation": "packages/models/README.md, the PyPI page, lists the package's capabilities and doesn't mention key_pool= or link docs/model-key-pool.md. The root README does link it (this diff). Adding one 'What you get' bullet would point a consumer reading PyPI at the guide. Optional.", "files": ["README.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The models dependency floor now sits among the argued floors, but its rationale argues only the two additions", "recommendation": "The entry moved from _RECORDED_AS_FOUND to _DEPENDENCY_FLOORS, which describes itself as 'an argued ruling, not an accretion'. Its rationale argues openai and httpx. The other twelve names are 'the floor recorded 2026-10-17', the as-found list that rationale says 'has not been argued'. The rationale also still says 'the 429-unretried hook', a name from before the rate-limit ruling (the hook is now the unretried_key_errors rule, and a rate-limit 429 is retried). Nothing reads either sentence, so no edit is needed for the merge. The next person to argue the models floor should know that only two of its names have been argued so far.", "files": ["tests/enforcement/test_dependency_alignment.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A pooled call that outlives reservation_margin is swept and logged as a crashed call's orphan", "recommendation": "Pooled reservations carry no deadline, so they expire 10 minutes after the call starts. A long stream still in flight is settled by the sweep, logged at WARNING and counted in threetears_key_pool_orphaned_reservations_total. docs/model-key-pool.md's Log lines table describes that line as 'the sweep found reservations a crashed call never closed'. The late settle corrects spend, but the count and the line stay. This is rare at the default margin, and the operator guide already says how expiry is computed. Something to know when reading the orphan counter.", "files": ["packages/models/src/threetears/models/key_pool/pool.py", "docs/model-key-pool.md"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Ledger account rows never expire", "recommendation": "Each (key, generation, period) gets its own coordination_ledger_accounts row, and the data model says these never expire. Growth is a few rows per key per month, and an account's orphaned map is bounded by its period. No lifecycle is needed at this volume.", "files": ["packages/core/src/threetears/core/coordination/budget_ledger.py"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: set covered, nothing clears a bar", "recommendation": "The pass covered all 15 files_oracle records: .claude/rules/learnings/core.md, api-contract, architecture, build-plan-model-key-pool, data-model, migration-scrub-decisions, observability-strategy, operational-spec, project-preferences, security-model, backlog.md, changes/model-key-pool.md, changes/prawduct-onboard-state.md, owner-rulings.md and project-state.yaml. I checked the change fragment's release and consumer-note claims against the code: the anthropic and openai floors and httpx; that exactly one of api_key and key_pool is required; credential_label on spans; breaker_registry, peek() and seconds_until_admit(); the refuses_retry statuses; and the cost-function scope. I also checked api-contract's key pool surface rows and data-model's two ledger rows. None of them ships a false claim, misleads an operator into action or gives a wrong instruction. The build plan's superseded chunk text (the 429-unretried mode, holds, the Q4 wait) carries a header saying it was replaced, so it misleads nobody.", "oid": "O-12"}], "duration_seconds": 960, "scope": "model-key-pool", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {}, "base_reviewed": "d0ce6f31cb6d33e33886e21754611cdb48b2633b", "record_lint": {"records": [".claude/rules/learnings/core.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-model-key-pool.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/migration-scrub-decisions.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-req